VALUES (
  '2026/10/01/hdq-thread-oct-1-2026',
  'thread',
  'thread',
  'Oil Gained 29% in the Third Quarter and the Canadian Dollar Ended It Where It Started: The Two-Year Yield Gap Is Setting the Currency',
  'WTI crude rose from $69.50 to $89.38 between June 30 and September 29 while USD/CAD moved from 1.4210 to 1.4188, and the widening gap between US and Canadian two-year yields tracks the currency far more closely than oil does.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Oil up 29%, dollar flat.</strong><span> WTI gained 28.6% from June 30 to September 29 while USD/CAD slipped 0.2%, from 1.4210 to 1.4188.</span></li>
<li><strong>The yield gap sets the path.</strong><span> Daily USD/CAD changes correlated +0.63 with changes in the US-Canada two-year gap and -0.22 with oil across 62 sessions.</span></li>
<li><strong>Both two-year yields sit about 100 basis points above policy.</strong><span> Canada is 112 basis points over the 2.25% overnight rate, the US about 101 over the midpoint of the 3.75% to 4.00% range.</span></li>
<li><strong>The currency leg changes sign with the window.</strong><span> USD/CAD fell 2.5% in July and August and rose 2.8% in September, so the S&amp;P 500 in Canadian dollars led the TSX by 5.2 points last month and 1.2 for the quarter.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The Canadian dollar ended the third quarter within 0.3% of where it began even though WTI crude gained 28.6% over the same 62 sessions, which means the currency is trading the gap between US and Canadian two-year yields and not the oil price. USD/CAD stood at 1.4210 on June 30 and 1.4188 on September 29, the last Bank of Canada observation of the quarter.</p>
<h2>Oil Carried the Hormuz Uncertainty and the Currency Carried Something Else</h2>
<p>WTI indexed to 100 on June 30 reached 152 on September 15 and finished at 129, while USD/CAD indexed the same way stayed between 97 and 100. Daily changes in USD/CAD correlated at 0.63 with daily changes in the US-Canada two-year yield gap and at -0.22 with daily WTI returns across those sessions (HDQ calculation).</p>
